Reference for the `sbc_hongkong` benchmark — Synthetic Business Cycle (SBC),
Shi, Xi & Xie (2025), "A Synthetic Business Cycle Approach to Counterfactual
Analysis with Nonstationary Macroeconomic Data," arXiv:2505.22388, Sec. 5.2.
Authors' code: github.com/jinxi-atlas/Synthetic-business-cycle-code (no licence).

Files
- reference.R       The authors' SBC_HK.R method (lsq detrending + trend_predict
                    forecast + Synth::synth ipop on the cycle and on levels),
                    run on the in-repo basedata/hong_kong_handover.csv. Needs
                    Synth and kernlab.
- golden_steps.R    The detrending half of the same run, emitted per step for
                    the unit tests in
                    mlsynth/tests/test_sbc_hongkong_reference.py. Base R only,
                    so it regenerates without CRAN:
                      Rscript benchmarks/reference/sbc_hongkong/golden_steps.R \
                        > benchmarks/reference/sbc_hongkong/golden_steps.txt
- golden_steps.txt  Its captured output (committed so the tests need no R).
                    Each quantity appears twice: the %.8f line, and a "hi:" line
                    at %.17g. The tests read the "hi:" line and compare at one
                    unit in its last recorded place, so the capture decides how
                    tight the comparison is. The %.8f form resolves 1e-8, three
                    orders coarser than the real difference between the
                    implementations.
- reference.json / reference.out / provenance.json / comparison.csv
                    Captured run of reference.R, version-pinned.

What the cross-validation found
- The detrending is the same computation on both sides. mlsynth's Hamilton
  filter reproduces the authors' lsq trend coefficients, the pre-handover cycle,
  the donor full-sample cycles and the trend_predict forecast to 2.6e-14 of each
  series' scale — the distance between R's lm QR (dqrls) and numpy's lstsq
  (gelsd), the same order as the German panel's 1.7e-14.
- Until golden_steps.R existed, this bundle captured only what Synth::synth
  produces (ATT, cyclical SSE, weights), so the case's detrending claim rested
  on a base-numpy rebuild of the authors' design rather than on R. Both checks
  now run: the numpy rebuild is a bitwise zero, and R is pinned per step.
- The synthetic-control step is the one divergence, and mlsynth is the more
  accurate implementation: the cyclical simplex least-squares is strictly
  convex, so its optimum is unique, and mlsynth attains it (cyclical SSE
  ~1.648e7, certified to 9.8e-8 relative by the convexity of the objective)
  while the authors' Synth::synth (kernlab ipop) converges to a point ~6% worse
  (~1.749e7) at any tolerance. Both recover an Italy/Germany-dominant cyclical
  synthetic Hong Kong and a negative post-handover effect.

The authors' code/data are reproduced here for offline replication of this
benchmark only; all credit to the authors.
